ion coefficients) of the candidate obesity gene, <italic>FTO</italic>, on brain atrophy in a cognitively normal adults and those with MCI and AD [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B76">76</xref>]. The figure is adapted from Ho et al. (2010) with kind permission from the authors and publishers.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJAD2011-490140.001"/></fig><table-wrap id="tab1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Top AD risk genes.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Gene</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Protein</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Population</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Polymorphism</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">APOE_e2/3/4</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">apolipoprotein E</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">all</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">APOE_e2/3/4*</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CLU</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">clusterin</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">all</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">rs11136000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">EXOC3L2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">exocyst complex component 3-like 2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">all</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">rs597668</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BIN1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">bridging integrator 1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">all</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">rs744373</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">